            <ArticleTitle>Effectiveness of acupuncture in patients with category IIIB chronic pelvic pain syndrome: a report of 97 patients.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>518-23</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/j.1526-4637.2009.00794.x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Chronic prostatitis/chronic pelvic pain syndrome (CP/CPPS) is of significant interest in urology and unfortunately, the therapy modalities recommended are not fully effective. Therefore, we undertook a pilot study to determine whether acupuncture improves the pain, voiding symptoms, and quality of life in men with category IIIB CP/CPPS.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Prospective, one-group trial, cohort study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Outpatient urology clinic.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS AND INTERVENTIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Ninety-seven CP/CPPS patients received six sessions of acupuncture to the BL-33 acupoints once a week. The National Institute of Health Chronic Prostatitis Symptom Index (NIH-CPSI) was completed by each patient before and after the treatment and on 12th and 24th weeks following the treatment.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Mean values of total CPSI score, pain subscore, urinary subscore, and quality of life subscore after the treatment and on follow-up after the treatment were compared with the baseline values.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">There was a statistically significant decrease in all of the subscores evaluated at all periods compared with the baseline. Eighty-six patients out of 93 (92.47%) were NIH-CPSI responders (more than 50% decrease in total NIH-CPSI score from baseline) at the end of the treatment.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The results of this study suggest that acupuncture appears to be a safe and potentially effective treatment in improving the symptoms and quality of life of men clinically diagnosed with CP/CPPS.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
